                <h2>History</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p> Ireland's national flag, known as the Bratach na hÉireann or the Irish tricolour, is a powerful visual encapsulation of the island's complex history, its aspirations for peace, and its evolving national identity. 
                    The flag consists of three equal vertical stripes—green at the hoist, white in the center, and orange at the fly. 
                    While simple in appearance, the symbolism embedded in these colors represents centuries of political tension, cultural transformation, and hopes for reconciliation. 
                    The green stripe traditionally symbolizes the Gaelic and Catholic community, tracing its roots to Ireland's native population and nationalist movements. 
                    This color became associated with Irish identity particularly during the late 18th century, when the Society of United Irishmen adopted green as a symbol of republican ideals and unity beyond sectarian divisions. 
                    The orange stripe, in contrast, reflects the Protestant and Unionist community, connected historically to William of Orange, whose victory at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690 shaped the political landscape for generations. 
                    The placement of the white stripe between green and orange symbolizes a desired peace and lasting harmony between these communities—an aspiration that has been tested repeatedly but remains central to Ireland's national narrative.</p>
                </div>
