                <h2>Tri Colour</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>
                    The tricolour first gained public visibility in 1848 when Thomas Francis Meagher, an Irish nationalist inspired by European revolutions, introduced it as a flag representing unity and equality between Ireland's religious and political groups. 
                    However, it would not become widely recognized as Ireland's national flag until much later, particularly during the Easter Rising of 1916, when revolutionaries raised the tricolour above the General Post Office in Dublin. 
                    Following the struggle for independence and the establishment of the Irish Free State in 1922, the flag became the official national emblem and remained so when the country later declared itself the Republic of Ireland in 1949. 
                    Today, the flag is flown on government buildings, used during international sports events, and cherished by Irish communities worldwide. 
                    Despite its nationalist origins, the flag's intended meaning is one of peaceful coexistence and mutual respect, reflecting Ireland's long journey toward reconciliation, especially in relation to Northern Ireland. 
                    The tricolour is not merely a national symbol but a representation of the hope that different traditions can share a future rooted in cooperation. 
                    Its significance continues to resonate both domestically and among the diaspora, reminding people of Ireland's unique cultural blend, historical struggles, and ongoing efforts to build bridges between diverse identities. 
                    In this way, the Irish flag stands not only as a marker of national pride but also as a beacon of the peace and unity that Ireland strives to uphold in the 21st century.
                    </p>
                    
